  "tree_id": 819,
  "family": "Hamamelidaceae",
  "memo": "&lt;em&gt;Liquidambar styraciflua&lt;/em&gt;, commonly known as sweetgum, is a large deciduous tree native to the eastern United States and Mexico. This species develops a straight bole and, at maturity, a broad, rounded crown. Its leaves are particularly distinctive, featuring five lobes with sharply acuminate tips and serrated margins. Renowned for its striking autumn coloration, sweetgum is a popular choice for street and landscape plantings. However, its vigorous root system can damage sidewalks and pavement, requiring ample planting space. Additionally, the tree produces spherical fruits, commonly referred to as “gum balls,” which fall in spring and may create maintenance issues. This species is resistant to oak root fungus.",
